/Contents (World History \055 Module 1\072 Industrialization and WWI\012\0121\056 The Industrial Revolution\072\012The Industrial Revolution began in Great Britain during the mid\05518th century\054 transforming agrarian handicraft economies into industrial manufacturing systems\056 Key catalysts included British coal deposits\054 iron access\054 capital accumulation\054 and legal protections for private property\056 Inventions such as James Watt\047s steam engine\054 the spinning jenny\054 and mechanized looms revolutionized textile production\056 Mass urbanization followed as agricultural laborers migrated into factory towns\054 creating new social classes and labor movements\056\012\0122\056 Causes of the First World War\072\012World War I \0501914\2051918\051 was caused by four long\055term systemic factors\072 Militarism\054 Alliances\054 Imperialism\054 and Nationalism \050MAIN\051\056 Europe was divided into two major military blocks\072 the Triple Entente \050France\054 Russia\054 Great Britain\051 and the Triple Alliance \050Germany\054 Austria\055Hungary\054 Italy\051\056 The immediate catalyst was the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand of Austria in Sarajevo on June 28\054 1914\054 by Gavrilo Princip\056 The crisis escalated through alliance obligations into a continent\055wide conflict\056\012\0123\056 Trench Warfare and End of WWI\072\012WWI was defined by static trench warfare along the Western Front\054 stretching across France and Belgium\056 Defensive technologies such as machine guns\054 barbed wire\054 and heavy artillery created deadly stalemates\056 Industrial warfare introduced toxic chemical gases\054 tanks\054 and combat aircraft\056 The United States entered the war in 1917 following unrestricted German submarine warfare\056 Fighting concluded with the Armistice on November 11\054 1918\054 leading to the 1919 Treaty of Versailles\056)
/DS (font\072 normal normal 9pt Helvetica\073text\055align\072left\073color\072\043000000)
